swer with incorrect reasoning. 1 The firm is productively efficient. 2 Accurate answer. 2 Since ATC = MC OR (from diagram) ATC is at its minimum (so the firm is productively efficient). (ii) determine whether Firm A is allocatively efficient. You must give a reason for your choice. [2] Level Marks 0 The work does not reach a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 Correct answer with no or with incorrect reasoning. 1 The firm is not allocatively efficient. 2 Accurate answer. 2 Since AR(P) is not equal to MC, the firm is not allocatively efficient. (iii) determine whether Firm A is producing at the profit-maximizing (loss-minimizing) level of output. You must give a reason for your choice. [2] Level Marks 0 The work does not reach a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 Correct answer with no or with incorrect reasoning. 1 The firm is not producing at the profit maximizing (loss minimizing) level of output. 2 Accurate answer. 2 Since MR is different from MC, the firm is not producing at the profit maximizing (loss minimizing) level of output. – 5 – N21/3/ECONO/HP3/ENG/TZ0/XX/M (d) Using Table 1, calculate the monthly profit or loss Firm A is making at the current level of output, Q1. [2] TR = 30 × 1500 = 45 000 TC = 32 × 1500 = 48 000 Any valid working is sufficient for [1]. π = 45 000 −48 000 = −$3000 An answer of –$3000 or 3000 without any valid working is sufficient for [1]. NB alternative calculation, which should be fully rewarded: π = (AR −ATC)Q = (30 −32)1500 = −$3000 (e) Using Table 1, calculate the total fixed costs incurred by Firm A at the current level of output, Q1. [2] AFC = 32 − 28 = 4 Any valid working is sufficient for [1]. 𝐹𝐹𝐹𝐹= 𝐴𝐴𝐴𝐴𝐴𝐴× 𝑄𝑄= 4 × 1500 = $6000 An answer of $6000 or 6000 without any valid working is sufficient for [1]. (f) Using your answer to part (e), calculate the average fixed costs if Firm A produces 2000 units of output per month instead of Q1 units. [1] 6000 2000 = 3 An answer of $3.00 or 3 is sufficient for [1]. OFR applies. – 6 – N21/3/ECONO/HP3/ENG/TZ0/XX/M (g) List two assumptions of the perfect competition model. [2] Level Marks 0 The work does not meet a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 Limited response 1 For one assumption. 2 Accurate response 2 For two assumptions. Assumptions: • large number of firms • a homogenous product • freedom of entry and exit (or no barriers) • perfect information • perfect factor mobility. (h) Explain why a loss-making perfectly competitive firm will shut down in the long run but may not shut down in the short run. [4] Level Marks 0 The work does not reach a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 The written response is limited. 1–2 If a firm is making economic losses in the long run, it is not earning normal profits (or is not earning enough to stay in business). Award [1] if the answer refers only to factors not being fixed in the long run. OR if it is making losses in the short run, it may not shut down because its revenue may cover its variable costs and thus is able to make a contribution to its fixed costs. An alternative response should clearly show that, as a result of fixed costs, the firm makes smaller losses by continuing to operate instead of shutting down. 2 The written response is accurate. 3–4 If a firm is making economic losses in the long run, it is not earning normal profits (or is not earning enough to stay in business). Award [1] if the answer refers only to factors not being fixed in the long run. AND if it is making losses in the short run, it may not shut down because its revenue may cover its variable costs and thus is able to make a contribution to its fixed costs. An alternative response should clearly show that, as a result of fixed costs, the firm makes smaller losses by continuing to operate instead of shutting down.","markschemeUrl":"https://ibdocs.re/p/IB%20PAST%20PAPERS%20-%20YEAR/2021%20Examination%20Session/November%202021%20Examination%20Session/Individuals%20and%20societies/E