40f18b489935354bbb883884ce1423ea4bb2036df9b1ae1f11de1c1","1b3e9f3617a8a0df1d489cdae2d540cfb09c4f967a759c44f5676ca06c9f0243","88db7748a2b4490e5bbdd34024a42da3d287e80b5b4ffeb77d2b62312c6ef788","6f62ae8e72cf131ec07586ccae8a8e89c5866fddf8726cc525f692924459cef8"]},"summary":"Financial-account, current-transfer, net-export, deficit-correction, administrative-barrier and PED calculations were misassigned across unrelated slots. Independent visual recheck of the printed marked parts and paired official markscheme pages supports the corrected scope."},"classificationProvenance":{"semanticQA":"modify","sourceMarkschemePdfSha256":"13ba554b5a60531511e4be60dc0a818562adf791004a301199906c6b30c131e6","sourceProvisionalFinal":"2021-november-none-hl-p3-q03","sourceQuestionPdfSha256":"09bc359e29767b1949e3a29a02798c56c929d96437fcae4e10f40486203795ca"},"classificationReviewStatus":"semantic_qa_approved","classificationVersion":"economics-release-taxonomy-2026-09-09-human-capital-v1","commands":[],"component":"P3","course":"IB Economics HL","courseEra":"legacy_pre_2022","curriculumVersion":"legacy_pre_2022","detailedSubtopics":["Balance-of-payments structure","Deficit correction","Trade protection instruments","PED concepts"],"displayPages":[15,16,17,18,19,20,21],"era":"legacy_pre_2022","id":"2021-november-none-hl-p3-q03","imageStatus":"original","marks":25,"markschemeImageHashes":["1c491f5f1398a1abf6cb90d23eb82e5a7d3e4e14835369daece06ff1a4616063","7179ef0ef934d4dd39f13aeb5c614712911fa77040df646920c70437cc172a9e","adfc5846f406e51c8f4dcca9f0f764b0931054e87b2a5d258270142fb0742944","55a71de6810945a723b323171cf74e5e20042b7d3b8e15bde7b2c6191670b421"],"markschemeImages":["markschemes/2021-november-none-hl-p3-q03/markscheme-01.webp","markschemes/2021-november-none-hl-p3-q03/markscheme-02.webp","markschemes/2021-november-none-hl-p3-q03/markscheme-03.webp","markschemes/2021-november-none-hl-p3-q03/markscheme-04.webp"],"markschemeTranscript":"3. (a) Distinguish between credit and debit items in the balance of payments. [2] Level Marks 0 The work does not meet a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 Limited understanding. 1 The idea that the distinction relates to inflows and outflows of currency. 2 Clear understanding. 2 A credit item represents an inflow of currency (+ve item) into the account/country, whereas a debit item represents an outflow of currency (–ve item) from the account/country. (b) State one example of a debit item from the financial account of the balance of payments. [1] Correct examples include: • (net) outward portfolio investment • (net) outward FDI • increase in forex reserves • (net) outflow of “hot money”. OR examples of any of the above, such as: Domestic residents purchasing foreign bonds / setting up a new company abroad or acquiring controlling percentage of shares of a foreign company / the central bank buying foreign currency / domestic residents buying foreign currency. For any correct example award [1]. (c) Using Table 5, calculate the value of net current transfers for Laylaland in 2020. [1] 539 – 1397 = −858 An answer of −$858 million or −858 is sufficient for [1]. (d) Using Table 5, calculate the net exports of goods and services for Laylaland in 2020. [2] NX + Net Income + Net Current Transfers= −1865 (Net Income= 412 − 1075 = −663) NX = 663 + 858 − 1865 Any valid working is sufficient for [1]. Valid working may constitute identifying the three figures (663, 858, 1865) regardless of their +/- signs. NX = −$344 million An answer of –$344 without any valid working is sufficient for [1]. OFR applies if current transfers have been calculated incorrectly in part (c). – 12 – N21/3/ECONO/HP3/ENG/TZ0/XX/M (e) Explain two methods that Laylaland’s government could use to correct the current account deficit. [4] Level Marks 0 The work does not meet a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 The written response is limited. 1–2 For a limited explanation of one method, award a maximum of [1]. For an accurate explanation of one method OR a limited explanation of two methods award a maximum of [2]. Methods may include: • expenditure switching policies/discouraging imports/ currency depreciation • expenditure reducing policies/contractionary macro policies • supply-side policies. 2 The written response is accurate. 3–4 For providing an accurate explanation of one method and a limited explanation of one other method award a maximum of [3]. For providing two accurate methods, award a maximum of [4]. Accurate explanations may include: • expenditure switching policies: trade barriers/protection (such as tariffs and non-tariff barriers) which will make imports more expensive / discourage imports, and thus increase net exports OR subsidies which will make exports more competitive and imports less attractive • weakening (depreciating) its own currency, which will make its imports more expensive and exports cheaper, which should increase net exports. • expenditure reducing policies, such as contractionary fiscal or monetary policies to reduce income growth and prices and thus consumption of imports. Lowering prices should also assist exports, which will increase net exports. • supply-side policies, eg deregulation, to increase competitiveness of domestically produced goods (lower production costs and prices), such as by introducing new technologies and by nurturing a more skilled work force, which will increase net exports. Any two methods which are well explained should be fully rewarded. These may be two separate supply-side policies or two separate expenditure switching policies (but not two types of trade protection) or two separate expenditure reducing policies. – 13 – N21/3/ECONO/HP3/ENG/TZ0/XX/M (f) List two administrative barriers that Nofiberland could have used to limit imports of chia seeds. [2] Level Marks 0 The work does not meet a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 Limited response 1 For one relevant barrier. 2 Accurate response 2 For two relevant barriers. Barriers may include: • safety related regulations • health standards • increased red tape (bureaucracy) • specific packaging requirements • any other reasonable response. (g) Calculate the price elasticity of demand for chia seeds in Nofiberland following the imposition of the tariff. [2] 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃= 29 −35 35 21 −15 15 Any valid working (correct %Δ Qd(X) or %Δ P(X), provided the formula is not inverted) is sufficient for [1]. However, simply stating the correct % (eg a denominator of 40%) is not sufficient. 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃= 0.43 𝑜𝑜𝑜𝑜−0.43 or − 3 7 An answer of 0.43 or −0.43, without any valid working is sufficient for [1]. If a % sign is used in the final answer, a maximum of [1] may be awarded. (h) Calculate the change in consumer expenditure on imported chia seeds in Nofiberland resulting from the imposition of the tariff. [2] (21 × 22) − (15 × 31) = 462 − 465 Any valid working is sufficient for [1]. = −$3 million An answer of −$3 million or 3 without any valid working is sufficient for [1]. – 14 – N21/3/ECONO/HP3/ENG/TZ0/XX/M (i) Calculate the total welfare loss resulting from the imposition of the tariff on chia seeds. [2] ൬3 × 6 2 + 6 × 6 2 ൰ Any valid working is sufficient for [1]. If the response provides a correct calculation of one relevant triangle, then [1] may be awarded. = $27 million An answer of $27 million or 27 without any valid working is sufficient for [1]. (j) Outline one reason why the imposition of the tariff would lead to a welfare loss. [2] Level Marks 0 The work does not meet a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 Limited understanding. 1 Because it leads to misallocation of resources OR the sum of CS plus PS is not being maximized OR there is a loss in CS/consumers pay a higher price/quantity consumed decreases. 2 Clear understanding. 2 Because chia seeds that could have been imported at a lower cost are produced domestically OR because domestic firms would produce more inefficiently than foreign firms OR chia seeds that are worth more than the cost of importing them are not being consumed OR because the loss in CS outweighs the gain in PS and tax revenue. (k) Describe the impact of the rise in demand for quinoa on the terms of trade of Proteinland. [1] For stating that the terms of trade have improved/risen/increased or that there has been a favourable movement [1]. (l) Explain how the increase in world demand for quinoa would likely affect the current account balance of Proteinland. [4] Level Marks 0 The work does not meet a standard described by the descriptors below. 0 1 The written response is limited. 1–2 For the idea that the current account balance would improve because of increased (net) exports. 2 The written response is accurate. 3–4 For providing an accurate explanation that the increase in demand would increase the price of quinoa [1] and increase (the quantity of) exports [1], which would increase the export revenue [1] and improve the current account balance (a deficit would decrease, or a surplus would increase) [1]. NB A response which refers to PED/decrease in quantity may be awarded a maximum of [3].","markschemeUrl":"https://ibdocs.re/p/IB%20PAST%20PAPERS%20-%20YEAR/2021%20Examination%20Session/November%202021%20Examination%20Session/Individuals%20and%20societies/Economics_paper_3__HL_markscheme.pdf","maxMarks":25,"number":3,"officialCodes":[{"canonical_id":"legacy_pre_2022/3.3","concept_level_applicability":"SL_and_HL","curriculum_era":"legacy_pre_2022","normalized_detail_id":null,"official_code":"3.3","source_levels":["HL"]},{"canonical_id":"legacy_pre_2022/3.1","concept_level_applicability":"SL_and_HL","curriculum_era":"legacy_pre_2022","normalized_detail_id":null,"official_code":"3.1","source_levels":["HL"]},{"canonical_id":"legacy_pre_2022/1.2","concept_level_applicability":"SL_and_HL","curriculum_era":"legacy_pre_2022","normalized_detail_id":null,"official_code":"1.2","source_levels":["HL"]}],"officialEra":"legacy_pre_2022","officialMarkscheme":{"imageHashes":["1c491f5f1398a1ab