nt.macroeconomics.economic-growth"],"part_id":"(a)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.macroeconomics.gdp-and-gni-measures"],"part_id":"(b) (i)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.macroeconomics.gdp-and-gni-measures"],"part_id":"(b) (ii)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.macroeconomics.gdp-and-gni-measures"],"part_id":"(b) (iii)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.macroeconomics.macroeconomic-equilibrium"],"part_id":"(c)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.microeconomics.movements-and-shifts-in-supply"],"part_id":"(d)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.global-economy.effects-of-exchange-rate-changes"],"part_id":"(e)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.global-economy.strategy-evaluation","content.global-economy.sustainable-development"],"part_id":"(f)"},{"detailed_subtopic_ids":["content.global-economy.strategy-evaluation"],"part_id":"(g)"}],"subtopics":["Strategy evaluation","Effects of exchange-rate changes","Sustainable development","Economic growth","GDP and GNI measures","Macroeconomic equilibrium","Movements and shifts in supply"],"summary":"2. Read the extracts and answer the questions that follow. Text D — Overview of Bhutan 1 Bhutan is a lower-middle income country in southern Asia. It is mountainous, water is plentiful, and 60 % of the territory is prote","timezone":"TZ1","viewerAvailable":true,"year":2025,"zone":"TZ1","officialCodeRefs":["new_first_assessment_2022/2.2","new_first_assessment_2022/3.1","new_first_assessment_2022/3.2","new_first_assessment_2022/3.3","new_first_assessment_2022/4.10","new_first_assessment_2022/4.5","new_first_assessment_2022/4.7"],"retrievalFacets":["facet.demand-and-supply","facet.development-measurement","facet.development-strategies","facet.exchange-rates","facet.macro-objectives","facet.national-income-and-activity","facet.sustainability"]},{"accessibleText":"1. (a) Explain the importance of income elasticity of demand (YED) for the primary, manufacturing and service sectors of the economy when real incomes are rising.\b [10] (b) Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that the consumption of demerit goods is best reduced through legislation and regulation.\b [15]","assessmentObjectives":["AO2","AO3"],"bankSlug":"ib-economics-hl","canonicalId":"2025-may-tz2-hl-p1-q01","classificationConfidence":"reviewed","classificationEvidence":{"finalArtifactSha256":"28088b8f6a4bfd7c1c18ed9b992e1556876cba7a5df16f33353d29d475a011fb","method":"paired_image","qaHashes":{"markscheme":["5bc15a5dc60c69ba4cd968c9b6bcf9e7d51b03d573c6b08ecf59f43cefb585ab","db36b61bc467a9ca845b4c97a6ca781966d5e3eb3de6493dd715e4670e67f540","f262b8fd3b9b9d8eb0fcb680678e537e0d30c592cc9865d30959f0c92a838bc1"],"question":["52755926d9849ff449b30cc449303fac307501afdb500737590c7243c61325a6"]},"summary":"Independent page-paired review of every declared Q/MS asset found printed part identifiers and official criteria consistent with primary content.microeconomics.direct-regulation-and-consumer-nudges; no unsupported detail, skill/context inflation, level/era drift, or subpart carryover required a change."},"classificationProvenance":{"semanticQA":"keep","sourceMarkschemePdfSha256":"b44e07996b6d30ca193b2d62a9bb9ba39fb864b8b606f65763735a4fc6363cfd","sourceProvisionalFinal":"2025-may-tz2-hl-p1-q01","sourceQuestionPdfSha256":"ec8b1b45698fcd0d705d8d18f7ad7f5abdecca2e45ee933bc9e34a0b5ca09ee1"},"classificationReviewStatus":"semantic_qa_approved","classificationVersion":"economics-release-taxonomy-2026-09-09-human-capital-v1","commands":["Explain","evaluate"],"component":"P1","course":"IB Economics HL","courseEra":"new_first_assessment_2022","curriculumVersion":"new_first_assessment_2022","detailedSubtopics":["Direct regulation and consumer nudges","Interventionist and merit-good strategies","Income elasticity of demand (YED)"],"displayPages":[3],"era":"new_first_assessment_2022","id":"2025-may-tz2-hl-p1-q01","imageStatus":"original","marks":25,"markschemeImageHashes":["5bc15a5dc60c69ba4cd968c9b6bcf9e7d51b03d573c6b08ecf59f43cefb585ab","db36b61bc467a9ca845b4c97a6ca781966d5e3eb3de6493dd715e4670e67f540","f262b8fd3b9b9d8eb0fcb680678e537e0d30c592cc9865d30959f0c92a838bc1"],"markschemeImages":["markschemes/2025-may-tz2-hl-p1-q01/markscheme-01.webp","markschemes/2025-may-tz2-hl-p1-q01/markscheme-02.webp","markschemes/2025-may-tz2-hl-p1-q01/markscheme-03.webp"],"markschemeTranscript":"1. (a) Explain the importance of income elasticity of demand (YED) for the primary, manufacturing and service sectors of the economy when real incomes are rising. [10] Answers may include: • Terminology: YED, primary, manufacturing and service sectors, real incomes. • Explanation: of how YED (income elastic and income inelastic/ high and low YEDs) affects the rate at which the demand for different goods and services increases as real incomes rise, and thus the relative sizes and composition of the primary, manufacturing (secondary) and service (tertiary) sectors of the economy; of how negative YED results in a decrease in demand for inferior goods and services as real incomes rise. • Diagram: showing income elastic, income inelastic and inferior goods on an Engel curve and/or a demand and supply diagram showing relevant shifts of the demand curve. Assessment Criteria Part (a) 10 marks Marks Level descriptor 0 • The work does not reach a standard described by the descriptors below. 1–2 • The response indicates little understanding of the specific demands of the question. • Economic theory is stated but it is not relevant. • Economic terms are stated but they are not relevant. 3–4 • The response indicates some understanding of the specific demands of the question. • Relevant economic theory is described. • Some relevant economic terms are included. 5–6 • The response indicates understanding of the specific demands of the question, but these demands are only partially addressed. • Relevant economic theory is partly explained. • Some relevant economic terms are used appropriately. • Where appropriate, relevant diagram(s) are included. 7–8 • The specific demands of the question are understood and addressed. • Relevant economic theory is explained. • Relevant economic terms are used mostly appropriately. • Where appropriate, relevant diagram(s) are included and explained. 9–10 • The specific demands of the question are understood and addressed. • Relevant economic theory is fully explained. • Relevant economic terms are used appropriately throughout the response. • Where appropriate, relevant diagram(s) are included and fully explained. – 4 – 2225 – 5203M (b) Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that the consumption of demerit goods is best reduced through legislation and regulation. [15] Answers may include: • Terminology: demerit good, legislation, regulation. • Explanation: of the advantages of legislation and regulation such as ease of implementation, direct method of control, forcing consumers to comply; forces the MPB curve to shift towards the MSB curve where MPB is above the MSB. • Diagram: use of any relevant diagram such as a negative consumption externality diagram or an indirect tax diagram (as an alternative type of government intervention). • Synthesis (evaluate): a challenge to the view in terms of the possible disadvantages of legislation and regulation and the relative effectiveness of using other methods such as indirect taxes, awareness creation/education, nudges, subsidizing less harmful substitute goods; consideration of the word “best”. • Examples: real-world examples of the consumption of demerit goods being reduced through legislation and regulation or through the use of other forms of government intervention. As the question requires an evaluation of “best”, a maximum of [12] should be awarded where no other policies are considered, as the question would not be fully addressed. For [13] or more, the synthesis and evaluation must be effective and balanced and there must at least be some mention of alternative policies. Examiners should be aware that can